covered vaccines are being rolled out  around the world  but a determined minority are choosing  to refuse them  in america around 30 of adults are yet  to take the jab but what if the choice  was taken out of their hands  governments and companies are currently  debating whether covert 19 vaccines  should become mandatory  as the debate becomes increasingly  polarized dividing families colleagues  and communities our correspondents  answer your questions on this topic  what is the argument for forcing people  to get vaccinated  i mean the argument for mandatory  vaccination is that you get better  coverage and that's good for public  health it's good for everyone's safety  and obviously protects people who can't  get vaccinated or who aren't protected  well by vaccines and what i would say is  that i think a certain amount of arm  twisting by governments is perfectly  justified  but when it goes too far  and um it becomes too coercive then what  worries me is that in infringes on  people's you know bodily autonomy and  right to decide what goes on with their  body  and it also  shows that you've kind of basically lost  the argument the public health argument  that these things are a good thing for  you to take  why is the debate becoming so polarized  this is a hugely divisive issue and yes  we're seeing it in workplaces we're  seeing it in families where um people  are arguing and trying to convince  family members to get vaccinated and  others are trying to convince them to  not get vaccinated we're seeing people  who are vaccinated claiming that  their rights are being infringed upon  right i can't go to yoga and know that  when i'm in the hot yoga room i'm not  breathing in coven i don't know if um i  go to the supermarket whether or not i  am safe and then other people are saying  well absolutely not this is an  infringement on our rights um you can't  force me to do anything that and put  anything unnatural into my body et  cetera et cetera so we are definitely  seeing in the u.s uh a big split it's  another example of extreme polarization  in the us and you know the us is facing  quite an uncertain future it's facing  uh lots and  more covert outbreaks in uh  states that um are struggling to deal  with these cases  um you know winter's not going to be  far off  and so i can see  why there's a lot of frustration  and anger and uh kind of push from the  vaccinated um to have the unvaccinated  you know roll up their arms  are covered vaccines mandatory anywhere  code vaccines by and large are not  mandatory although it will depend where  you are in the world and uh what job you  do as well um and also it's changing uh  as we speak people are formulating um  new policies on this all the time  um italy was the first european country  to mandate uh coveted vaccines for  healthcare workers and i think as we  look forward we'll see lots of  healthcare workers being pressured by  governments to get vaccines  and in the us we were seeing this really  interesting pushback against vaccine  mandates before they were even  implemented many states had passed laws  against  mandatory vaccinations states like  florida but with the the latest wave of  covid uh cases we're seeing that  many other many state governments many  city governments as well as private  institutions are starting to mandate  those vaccines we're seeing that the  federal government is mandating  vaccination or regular testing among  federal employees we're seeing states  like california and virginia require it  of their employees  new york city is also requiring it of  its city employees  private companies like walmart are also  requiring vaccination  have vaccines ever been made mandatory  in the past  the first vaccine that was ever made  mandatory was the smallpox vaccine and  that happened in britain and that was in  1853  and  children were required to be vaccinated  and there was a fine imposed which would  be equivalent to about 180 today  and what was really interesting about  this is it stimulated the rise of the  anti-vaccination movement a really  strong  uprising a really strong movement that  actually spread overseas and the  arguments are all very similar to what  we hear today a lot of the hysterical  misinformation so we do have to be  careful when we make vaccines um  mandatory that we don't kind of  stimulate this you know huge outcry at  what is seen as to be  an unfair uh situation  do incentive programs to encourage  vaccinations work  so we've seen a lot of really  interesting incentive programs in the us  some states like ohio were  have had a lottery for  one million dollar  uh checks to people who got vaccinated  we also saw a hundred dollar gift card  incentives in new york city and of  course we saw  retailers giving away free donuts and  other things as well  um  unfortunately a recent study found  that looks particularly at ohio they  found that the lotteries were not  actually  incentivizing people to get the vaccine  so it looks like they might not be  working but mayor bill de blasio of new  york city thinks that his 100 incentive  system did increase vaccinations in  early august so my favorite uh was in a  philippine town the mayor is planning a  cow raffle  um but we've seen all sorts of things  being offered from uber lifts  you know in lebanon um to discounted  takeaway meals so really it depends on  the country you're in as to what the  government is prepared to offer you  but you know as tamara said um  you know there's a limited extent to  which these are going to work they will  work for a few people  but they won't work for everyone  [Music]  can employers make vaccines mandatory  you know what's possible will really  depend on what country you're in some  countries like canada it's certainly  quite hard uh in britain if you are  hiring new staff then certainly that  does appear to be the case um i know  that employers here do want to do this  um for existing staff um and i think  what we will see in britain and in other  countries is that this will get tested  legally  as employers bring this in and in the  united states state governments and  private employers can definitely mandate  vaccines there are three exceptions that  tend to come into play there's um  personal beliefs religion as well as  medical exceptions and in some states  like california new york they don't even  actually allow for a religious exemption  or a personal belief exemption so  it's actually quite surprising when you  dig in  do you think governments will ever make  covert vaccines mandatory well we know  that they have in saudi arabia we know  that the moscow government  has rolled out a mandatory vaccination  program for  service sector workers so i think it's  clear that some governments will do this  but in other places citizens just won't  accept it and it won't happen  it is not  constitutional for the federal  government to require all u.s citizens  to get a vaccine what would be more  likely to happen is that state  governments require it in  their to varying degrees and as we can  see currently that will depend that  would depend widely on the politics of  the state we're seeing that while some  states like california are requiring  vaccination among public employees other  states  are banning like florida are banning  mass in schools or anywhere are  are banning vaccine passports so  based on the politics and of course the  legal constraints we're not likely to  see a vaccine mandate in the entire  united states  i'm natasha loder the health policy  editor of the economist  you can read more of our coverage on  kobit 19 by clicking on the link  thanks for watching and don't forget to  subscribe  [Music]
 

